{
  "id": "dr_watts_lined_hymnody",
  "name": "Dr. Watts hymnody (African American lined-out hymns)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Congregational hymn practice of Black Baptist and other Protestant churches, above all in the devotional service that opens worship: a deacon or leader chants out a line or couplet of a hymn by Isaac Watts or another English hymn writer, and the congregation answers by singing it very slowly in long meter. It continues the lining out of 17th- and 18th-century British and colonial churches, transformed in African American worship into long, melismatic, heterophonic singing without instruments; Library of Congress field recordings and William T. Dargan's study Lining Out the Word document it.",
  "recipe": "classic modern dr. watts hymnody, ritual Devotional, American sacred branch. leader call answered, many notes per syllable, same melody all voices. country church. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
